## PHASE 2: THE ROTATION (ONLY on Wednesday's separate GO after your PHASE 1 STATUS B)
**Until the GO ROTATE you do the read-only measuring only, and you do not read the secret's value.** The new password is **HEX ONLY**: generated ON the box by `openssl rand -hex 24` (48 characters; never base64: gate 378 finding F2, `/Volumes/DevMASTER/WEDNESDAY/0_Brain/reference/2026-11-17_gate378-tier1-pr2252-ks2253-redis-url-mask/verdict.md`: a base64 password can hold `/`, `?` or `#`, which break ioredis URL parsing and were logged whole before the mask fix; and it satisfies Kam's "letters and numbers only"). The secret is NEVER printed, echoed, mailed, logged, committed, passed through the Mac or written anywhere except the box's own secret location (the stack env file the compose reads, mode preserved). You prove the rotation by service health and by a log line that shows the MASK, never the value.

### What you MEASURE FIRST, read-only, for the plan (STATUS B section "ROTATION PLAN")
1. **Where Kintsugi's Redis password is set.** Drafter's reading of the repo at the pin (`gh api .../contents/Blockchain/Dev/docker-compose.yml?ref=06328efa...`, saved `_work/compose_06328.yml`, key names only): `redis` service: `command: redis-server --appendonly yes --requirepass ${REDIS_PASSWORD} --maxmemory 256mb --maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru`; its healthcheck is `redis-cli -a "${REDIS_PASSWORD:-<repo default>}" ping` (NOTE: the repo has a built-in default for the healthcheck only; measure whether Kintsugi's env actually defines REDIS_PASSWORD, so the rotation is not silently a no-op against a default); data volume `redis_data`; port published on 127.0.0.1 only. Measure on the box, by NAMES and HASHES only: the stack env file(s) the compose uses for project `dev` (D 71st's envcopy shows a stack env of 16,894 bytes, mode 600, carried `evidence/kintsugi/b3_envcopy.txt`; find the real paths: `.env`, `.env.local` (auth declares `env_file: .env.local, required: false`, carried `evidence/compose_env_read_pin.txt`), anything under `Dev/` or `/home/secuura/`), how many lines name `REDIS_PASSWORD` or `REDIS_URL` in each (a COUNT, never the line), and whether any file overrides the compose's value. Count the occurrences with a control. **A stale copy exists: `/home/secuura/seatD71-envcopy` (D 71st's copy of the stack env, mode 600, carried HANDOVER item 6) holds the OLD password and any other copy of the env does too; list every such copy by path and mode, and put its handling in the plan (it is a second place the old value lives; do not delete it, ask).**
2. **Every service that reads REDIS_URL or REDIS_PASSWORD.** Drafter's reading of the compose at the pin (`_work/redis_readers.txt`, from `awk` over `compose_06328.yml`; all 21 lines have the shape `REDIS_URL=redis://:${REDIS_PASSWORD...}@redis:6379` and 21 is `grep -c` of that shape): **analytics, anchoring, api-gateway, auth, billing, demo-service (demo's; in the compose but NOT running on Kintsugi's rotation set: check whether Kintsugi runs it and say), governance, guardian, kyc, m365-integration, nft-certificate, originate, prism, queue, referral, security, staking, timestamping, transfer, vc-issuer, wallet-connector**; plus the `redis` server itself and its healthcheck. No service reads a separate `REDIS_HOST` or `REDIS_PASSWORD` (the only other uses are the server's `--requirepass` and its healthcheck). **Verify against the running containers by key NAME only** (`docker inspect -f '{{range .Config.Env}}{{println .}}{{end}}'` is FORBIDDEN, it prints values; use a reader that prints the variable NAME and `case`-tests presence, as D 71st's `walletkeys` tool did). Also which of these have `depends_on: redis` and which treat Redis as REQUIRED (the gateway's production guard has `REDIS_REQUIRED_IN_PRODUCTION`; gate 378 Q2 read it): the plan must say what each does when its Redis login is refused (crash, retry, degrade). Read the code, do not probe.
3. **What Redis holds and what a restart costs.** Redis runs with `--appendonly yes` and a volume, `allkeys-lru`, 256 MB. Measure (counts only, by an in-container `redis-cli` run with the login taken from the container's own environment or a protected file, never from your command line, and never printing it): `DBSIZE`, key-prefix class counts (rate limits, sessions, token revocation, queue/bull, caches), `INFO persistence` fields (`aof_enabled`, `loading`). The plan must say what is lost, if anything, when Redis restarts, and whether any live job queue (`queue` service) has pending work.
4. **The restart order that avoids a window where clients hold the old password.** Two candidate sequences; the seat measures which one the box and Redis 8 support and proposes ONE; Wednesday rules:
   - **SEQ-A (no refusal window, preferred if it works):** (i) generate the new value on the box into a protected scratch file mode 600 (never printed); (ii) write it to the stack env file in place of the old value, SURGICALLY: exactly one matching line (count it, refuse on any count but 1), the file's other bytes unchanged (prove with a hash of the file with that one line masked, before and after), mode and owner preserved, the old file kept as `<env file>.pre-<tag>` in the same directory, mode 600 (the rollback, on the box only, until the watch window passes); the running containers still hold the old value in their environment, so nothing changes yet; (iii) on the running Redis, ADD the new value as a second accepted login for the default user at runtime (Redis ACL: `ACL SETUSER default >NEW`, which keeps the old accepted, run in-container from the protected file), verify BOTH logins work by exit status only; (iv) force-recreate the clients ONE AT A TIME (they read the new value from the env at creation), health-gated each, in an order that puts edge-critical services last (gateway and auth last, as in D 71st); (v) recreate `redis` itself so it takes `--requirepass NEW` from the env (the ACL addition is runtime-only and the restart drops it; AOF reloads the data); a few seconds of connection errors on every client are expected at that one moment and the clients must reconnect: measure it; (vi) verify the OLD login is now refused (`WRONGPASS` or `NOAUTH` by exit status) and the new one accepted, every client healthy with restarts 0, the gateway's new `[Redis] Connecting` line showing the mask. Caveat to measure FIRST: whether `ACL SETUSER default >NEW` really leaves `requirepass`'s password valid alongside on this Redis version, tested on a THROWAWAY, loopback-only, empty `redis:8-alpine` container (same image id as the box's `redis`) with a made-up value, NOT on Kintsugi's Redis; that is the one allowed write outside the deploy, declare it in STATUS A and remove nothing else.
   - **SEQ-B (brief stop-the-world, fallback):** write the env file as above, then recreate `redis` and all 21 clients in one batch order (redis first, wait healthy, then clients, edge last). A window of refused logins on every client from the redis recreate until each client's own recreate; measure its length from D 71st-style `healthwin` runs (carried client swap ~4-6.5 s each) and state it in seconds.
   Either way, ONE window is unavoidable: Redis's own restart. Say what it costs.
5. **Tooling gap (trap h):** the carried swap driver refuses redis in its set. Say how you will drive the recreates (a NEW minimal driver with the same validations: only the named services, never postgres, redis only in step (v), health-gated, nonfinal-gate (D 71st's `NONFINAL`) before the first step, a log with timestamps), or ask Wednesday to rule an alternative. Build and dry-run it during Phase 1; run it only on the GO ROTATE.
6. **Rollback (Phase 2):** the old value kept ON THE BOX ONLY, in the same protected location (`<env file>.pre-<tag>`, mode 600), until the watch window passes (+15 min, then Wednesday's word; do not delete it yourself). Rollback recipe = restore that file over the env file (surgical, hashed) and repeat the same sequence in reverse (Redis takes `--requirepass OLD`, clients recreated). Prove the recipe's first step on the file copy, dry, without touching the live file.
7. **Other places the old password is used:** any script, cron, backup job, compose override or monitoring probe on the box that logs in to Redis from outside the compose set (names and paths only). `grep -c` over the box's scripts for `REDIS_PASSWORD` with a control; a hit is a plan line, not a print.
8. **Proof plan (what STATUS B for the rotation will show, none of it a value):** env-file hash-with-line-masked EQUAL before and after; new login accepted and old refused (exit statuses); all 21 clients healthy, restarts 0; the gateway's NEW `[Redis] Connecting` line masked (the same boolean-guarded read as Phase 1); no `WRONGPASS` or `NOAUTH` storm in any client's log after the settle (counts since each client's recreate, with a control); KS-535 `695d09df873ff42b` on anchoring and nft-certificate, anchoring StartedAt EQUAL (anchoring and nft-certificate are in the set of 21 and DO get recreated for the rotation: so `StartedAt` EQUAL cannot hold after Phase 2; the equality to prove then is the KS-535 value and the wallet key NAMES, and Wednesday must rule whether those two are recreated, see QUESTIONS Q3); +15 min watch on every recreated service.

